# coverage-exclude — lines devtools/coverage must not count, and why.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FILE IS THE REASONS, NOT THE EXCLUSIONS.
#
# A coverage floor of 65% says "some of this is untested and we have agreed not
# to say which". A floor of 100% against an explicit exclusion list says "every
# line is either executed by the suite or listed here with a reason a reviewer
# can reject". The second is a hard cut; the first is a mood. Moving to the
# second is only honest if this file stays small, specific, and argued.
#
# FORMAT
#   path/to/file              # reason        whole file
#   path/to/file:120-260      # reason        an inclusive line range
#
# Every entry needs a reason. An entry without one is an error, not a default.
#
# SELF-CHECKING. devtools/coverage fails if any excluded line was actually
# EXECUTED by the suite. That makes the file impossible to use for hiding live
# code behind a stale range: the moment a test reaches an excluded line, the
# exclusion has to go. It does not stop someone excluding code that ought to be
# tested — nothing mechanical can — which is why each entry has to argue.
#
# LINE-PINNED RANGES ROT BY DESIGN (review 2026-08-15T1752Z §5). Any edit to
# an excluded file shifts its ranges, and devtools/coverage then fails — at
# ed82166 it failed against its own HEAD for exactly this reason. That is the
# accepted cost, not a defect to engineer away: an exclusion that tracked its
# lines automatically could silently keep hiding code as the file changed
# underneath it, which is the abuse the self-check exists to make impossible.
# The rule when it fires: re-derive the range from the current file, re-read
# the reason, and drop the entry if the reason no longer holds. Expect to
# touch this file in the same commit as any edit to a file listed below.

# ── 1. Cannot appear in their own trace ──────────────────────────────────
#
# These RUN the suite. devtools/coverage starts tests/run-tests under xtrace
# and reads the trace back; a line of the harness executes before or after that
# child process, never inside it. Measuring them requires a second harness
# measuring the first, which is a regress, not a test.
devtools/coverage           # runs the suite under trace; cannot trace itself
devtools/awk-coverage       # runs the suite under gawk --profile; same regress
devtools/check              # runs repo-validate and the suite
devtools/assert-test-catches  # runs the suite once per mutation, by design
reviews/verify.sh           # runs the suite and coverage to check the register

# ── 2. Gated, and gated on purpose ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# tests/system-roundtrip installs to the real system and reverts it. It runs
# only with BT_SYSTEM_TEST=1 and only when nothing is already installed,
# because running it by accident on the investigation machine would stop the
# watchdog mid-experiment. The gate is the feature; the suite must not defeat
# it to raise a number.
tests/system-roundtrip      # CI-gated install/revert; must not run by accident

# devtools/journal-contract WAS excluded whole, on the argument that it is a CI
# step in its own right and folding it in would double the suite's runtime. That
# argument covered its phase 2, which builds a real journal with
# systemd-journal-remote — and it silently covered phase 1 as well, which is
# pure output-shape checking against whatever journalctl is on PATH.
#
# Phase 1 had therefore never executed under test, and on 2026-08-16 it reported
# the journal contract BROKEN while journalctl was behaving normally: it read
# the first 5000 lines of `-b all` hunting a boot separator, and the
# investigation machine's oldest boot is a dyndbg tracing boot of 1,586,655
# kernel lines. The suite now drives phase 1 against a stub modelling exactly
# that host, which costs milliseconds and would have caught it.
#
# The exclusion is gone rather than narrowed to phase 2. Phase 2 declines on its
# own when systemd-journal-remote is absent, which is the honest mechanism and
# does not need a line here.

# ── 3. The suite's own failure branches ──────────────────────────────────
#
# Every assertion in tests/run-tests is `ok "..."` or `bad "..."`. In a green
# run the `bad` branch of every passing check is by construction unexecuted —
# 72 lines of it. Covering them means failing the suite, and a suite that must
# fail to be fully covered has the wrong incentive at its centre.
#
# NOT excluded wholesale. The first draft of this entry named the whole file,
# and the self-check refused it immediately: 1200 executed lines would have
# left the denominator while staying in the numerator. Only the failure
# branches go, matched by SHAPE rather than by line number, so a `bad` added
# tomorrow is covered and a list of numbers cannot rot.
tests/run-tests:/^(\|\| *)?bad /   # failure branches: unreachable in a green run

# ── 4. Another language, measured by another tool ────────────────────────
#
# bash traces COMMANDS. A multi-line awk program is one command: bash reports
# its first line and nothing else, so every remaining line counts as uncovered
# for as long as the program exists. Those lines are not untested — they are
# measured by devtools/awk-coverage, which reads gawk --profile and reports
# statement coverage, and which is gated in CI in its own right.
#
# WHICH LINE OF THE CONSTRUCT IS TRACED DEPENDS ON THE CONSTRUCT, and getting
# that wrong is how the first draft of this section was wrong twice.
#
#   a multi-line SIMPLE command       — traced at its FIRST line
#   a multi-line COMMAND SUBSTITUTION — traced at its LAST
#
# `bt_journal … | awk '…' >> "$F"` reports the line the PIPELINE starts on;
# `indices=$(python3 -c '…' <<<"$j")` and a bare `awk '…' "$TMP"` both report
# the line they CLOSE on. So a range leaves out whichever end bash reports,
# and which end that is depends on the construct.
#
# No attempt is made to state the rule exhaustively here, because two attempts
# to do so were both wrong. The self-check is the arbiter: propose a range,
# run devtools/coverage, and it names any line that is excluded and executed.
# Every entry below has been through that loop. That is the useful property —
# not that the rule was derived correctly, but that a wrong range cannot
# survive a single run.
# Two of these five ranges were wrong when written by eye, and the self-check
# refused both: bt-sco's started three lines late and sanitize-logs.sh's
# swallowed the awk-capability probe, which is live shell that runs on every
# invocation. They are now derived from where each quoted program actually
# opens and closes.
tools/bt-actions:85-267     # inline classifier; see devtools/awk-coverage
tools/bt-actions:280-287    # inline MGMT-intent extractor; same
tools/bt-sco:115-123        # inline request/completion block filter; same
tools/bt-capdiff:88-108     # inline record extractor; same
tools/sanitize-logs.sh:142-191  # inline substituter and its verifier; same
tools/bt-boot-list:69-74    # inline JSON boot-index reader; same
tools/bt-env-history:82-91  # inline metrics.tsv column reader; same
tools/bt-logvolume:110-114  # inline message normaliser; same
tools/bt-logvolume:116-121  # inline share renderer (same pipeline, second program)
tools/bt-trial:250-254      # inline trial-number counter; same
tools/bt-trial:702-711      # inline sco-params window filter; same
tools/bt-verify-install:57-60   # inline install_file parser; same
tests/run-tests:259-264       # inline --section extractor; same

# THE ELEVENTH ENTRY WAS MISSING AND COST MORE THAN A PERCENTAGE POINT.
# devtools/test-comprehension filters `exit N` inside an embedded program out of
# a unit's refusal count, and it uses THIS FILE as the filter rather than a
# second guess about where quoting starts. So a program body that is absent here
# is not merely counted as uncovered lines — its awk `exit 1` is counted as a
# refusal of the shell tool that nothing has ever driven, and cannot be, because
# no test can make bash trace a line inside another language. That pinned
# tools/bt-verify-kernel-mechanism at 50% against a floor of 75, permanently and
# for a reason no amount of testing could remove.
tools/bt-verify-kernel-mechanism:135-144  # inline byte-aligned device-id matcher; same

# Derived by scanning for quoted programs that open on one line and close on
# another, NOT read off by eye — the two entries written by hand were both
# wrong, and the self-check caught both. Whole-file entries above already
# cover devtools/coverage's and devtools/awk-coverage's own inline programs.

# ── 5. Reachable only from an installed machine, not from a checkout ─────
#
# These three branches exist FOR the installed layout — bt-health-report at
# /usr/local/bin with no repository beside it — and a checkout cannot take
# them, because the very files they fall back from are present in a checkout.
#
# tests/run-tests DOES exercise all three, by copying the script and its lib
# into a temp tree with no evidence/ beside them. That is the honest test and
# it is worth having. It cannot close these lines: the trace records the copy
# under an absolute temp path, and devtools/coverage counts only
# repository-relative paths — deliberately, so a tool's installed image is
# never confused with its source.
# Single lines are written as one-line RANGES. `path:61` alone parses as
# 61-0 and silently matches nothing — which is a quiet no-op, the one failure
# an exclusion file must not have, since it looks exactly like an exclusion
# that worked.
tools/bt-health-report.sh:67-67    # install stamp: only present on an installed machine
tools/bt-health-report.sh:70-70    # /etc unit mtime: only when the stamp is absent AND the unit installed
tools/bt-health-report.sh:86-86    # --list-boots fallback: only when bt-boot-list is not beside the script

# THE FOURTH ENTRY POINTED AT A BLANK LINE, and had done since it was written —
# `:212-212` when 212 was blank, not a range that rotted. So it excluded nothing,
# silently, while reading as a considered decision. That is the failure this
# file's header warns about, reached by a different route than the `:61` parse
# trap it names: a syntactically valid range over dead space. A no-op exclusion
# is worse than a wrong one, because nothing can disagree with it — the
# self-check only fires on lines that are excluded AND executed, and this was
# neither.
#
# Its REASON was wrong too. "a checkout always ships one" is not why the line is
# unreachable — tests/run-tests reaches the branch, by running a copy of the
# script in a temp tree with BT_SHARE_DIR and BT_HEALTH_DIR pointed at nothing.
# It is unreachable for the reason the three entries above are: the trace records
# that copy under an absolute temp path, and coverage counts only
# repository-relative ones. Same cause, so it belongs with them.
tools/bt-health-report.sh:228-228  # no-baseline branch: exercised only through the temp-tree copy

# ── 6. The damage report, and the differ it prints ───────────────────────
#
# Added with the /usr/bin footprint check, which fingerprints the system
# binaries before the run and compares after. Two entries, one argued each way.
#
# THE INLINE AWK is the differ's body — another language, measured here by
# neither instrument, like the entries in section 4. It differs from those in
# one respect worth stating: it is not merely "tested elsewhere", it is pinned
# by an assertion in tests/run-tests that feeds it synthetic footprints and
# compares its exact output, covering the appeared, rewritten and removed
# branches on every run. Mutating any one of the three turns that assertion red
# — verified by doing it. The range starts one line INSIDE the program because
# the construct is a pipeline, which bash traces at the line it opens on.
tests/run-tests:334-340     # inline footprint differ; driven and pinned by the assertion above

# THE REPORTING LOOP runs only when a binary actually changed, which on an
# undamaged machine is never — the same category as the `bad` lines section 3
# excludes by shape, and excluded here by number only because the shape rule
# matches lines that BEGIN with `bad` and this loop calls it mid-line. Its
# argument, the differ, is driven directly by the assertion above, so what is
# unreachable is the printing and not the logic.
tests/run-tests:461-462     # damage report: reached only when /usr/bin actually changed

# THE SAME REPORT FOR THE TRACKED TREE, added with the fixture check. Reached
# only when the suite actually mutated a tracked file, which on a correct run is
# never — the same category as the /usr/bin one directly above, and as the `bad`
# lines section 3 excludes by shape.
#
# The entry above ROTTED when that check was inserted: the range moved from
# 404-405 to 461-462 and devtools/coverage refused, naming it as an exclusion
# that matched nothing. That is the header's stated design working, and the
# reason both are re-derived here rather than widened.
tests/run-tests:436-437     # tracked-tree damage report: reached only when a fixture actually changed

# ── 7. bt-window's two counting passes ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Same case as section 4 — an inline awk body inside a pipeline, which bash
# traces at the line the pipeline opens on. These two replaced four separate
# journalctl scans that re-read identical ranges; the counts they produce are
# asserted directly by the fixture-driven case in tests/run-tests (3 timeouts,
# 1 USB line, 0 interventions), and the SHAPE that made the tool time out on a
# 14-hour boot is asserted separately by counting its journal reads.
tools/bt-window:88-90       # inline timeout/USB counter; counts asserted from a fixture
tools/bt-window:110-112     # inline intervention counter; same

# ── 8. bt-retention's two boot-range readers ─────────────────────────────
#
# Both loops that build the boot table are `done < <(cmd)` as the LAST
# statement of an `if`. Section 4's rule says that form IS traced, and on its
# own it is — a `done < <(f)` at top level reports its own line. Inside an `if`
# it does not: bash reports the process substitution at the `fi` BELOW it, and
# `fi` is a structural line the scanner never counts. So the line is executed,
# and there is no trace record that can ever say so.
#
# Verified rather than reasoned: running bt-retention under the same PS4 the
# coverage tool uses puts `python3 -c ...` at line 95 and `bt_journal
# --list-boots` at line 110, which are the two `fi`s.
#
# BOTH LOOPS ARE DRIVEN, which is why this is an instrument gap and not a
# hiding place. tests/run-tests reads the JSON form through
# tests/journal/retention and the text form through tests/journal/retention-text
# — two fixtures describing the same three boots by different routes — and
# asserts the two reports are byte-identical. Mutating the text parser's field
# positions or removing its em-dash normalisation turns that assertion red;
# both were checked by doing it.
tools/bt-retention:88-94    # inline JSON boot-range reader; the trace lands on the fi at 95
tools/bt-retention:109-109  # text-form reader's process substitution; trace lands on the fi at 110
